WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.954 --> 00:00:05.839
In this tutorial, we'll give our story a look: reference images for its characters

2
00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:08.579
and places, and a keyframe for the first shot.

3
00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:11.267
Open the Ingredients tab.

4
00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:17.035
These came from the story bible: characters, locations, props, and the overall

5
00:00:17.035 --> 00:00:17.479
style.

6
00:00:17.479 --> 00:00:20.742
Each card has a visual prompt that describes how it looks.

7
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.770
At the top, pick an image model, and how many candidates to make per run.

8
00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:31.270
FLUX dev, quantized to fit a sixteen gigabyte GPU, is a great default.

9
00:00:33.044 --> 00:00:34.956
Click Generate on a character.

10
00:00:34.956 --> 00:00:36.906
The job runs on your own GPU.

11
00:00:36.906 --> 00:00:42.376
You can follow its progress right on the card, with a live preview of each image as

12
00:00:42.376 --> 00:00:42.969
it forms.

13
00:00:50.302 --> 00:00:53.714
The first image becomes the character's primary reference.

14
00:00:53.714 --> 00:00:56.139
If you prefer another candidate, just click it.

15
00:00:56.139 --> 00:00:58.252
You can also upload a picture of your own.

16
00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:04.468
Let's make a reference for the location too, so the keyframe can put the character

17
00:01:04.468 --> 00:01:05.551
in the right setting.

18
00:01:13.716 --> 00:01:16.666
Now open the Shots board, and click the first shot.

19
00:01:18.922 --> 00:01:23.939
The Keyframe section lists the ingredient images it will use as references:

20
00:01:23.939 --> 00:01:27.684
characters first, then props, then the scene's location.

21
00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:30.988
Click Compose keyframe.

22
00:01:30.988 --> 00:01:36.542
LocalFlow places the references side by side on a reference sheet, and FLUX Kontext

23
00:01:36.542 --> 00:01:40.088
paints a single new frame that keeps each one's look.

24
00:01:49.282 --> 00:01:52.282
Click the candidate to make it the shot's keyframe.

25
00:01:52.282 --> 00:01:56.869
The shot is now marked keyframe ready, and the board shows the image.

26
00:01:57.447 --> 00:02:03.209
For experiments outside the story, the Playground runs any installed model directly.

27
00:02:03.209 --> 00:02:08.959
Pick a model, add LoRAs with their weights, and set a seed to reproduce a result

28
00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:09.534
exactly.

29
00:02:11.032 --> 00:02:11.757
That's it.

30
00:02:11.757 --> 00:02:17.620
Every shot can now start from a consistent keyframe, ready to be turned into video.
